    "result_text": "Done. The build passes with all tests green (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary of changes\n\n**New identity key.** `OwnerMapper.identityKey(owner)` now returns the lower-case SHA-256 hex digest (64 hex chars) of `normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)`, replacing the old plaintext `telephone|email|householdId`.\n\n**New helper class** `mapper/OwnerIdentity.java` holds `sha256Hex(...)` and an American `soundex(...)` implementation. I put these in a standalone utility rather than as `default` methods on the `@Mapper` interface because MapStruct treated the `String -> String` defaults as ambiguous candidate property-mapping methods (which broke the build).\n\n**Soft-match redesign.** `assignPossibleDuplicate` now flags a possible duplicate when an existing non-deleted owner has the **same postcode** and **same last-name Soundex** but a **different identity key** (previously: same normalized last name + same postcode + different telephone). Because telephone is part of the identity key, two owners with the same last name and postcode but different telephones no longer collide on the key \u2014 they are admitted as a soft match instead of a hard 409.\n\n**Duplicate detection (409)** in `rejectDuplicateIdentity` is unchanged in structure \u2014 it still compares the single `identityKey`, still skips soft-deleted owners, and the email-domain blocklist still runs first (in `normalizeEmail`, before the check). The separate household-duplicate 409 was already consolidated into this single key; `householdId` is still computed for household size / membership-cap but no longer feeds the identity key.\n\nI also removed the now-unused `toE164OrNull` helper and refreshed the Javadoc in the controller, `DuplicateOwnerIdentityException`, and `ExceptionControllerAdvice` to describe the new key format.",
